{{Japanese name|Watanabe}}

{{nihongo|'''Michio Watanabe'''|渡部 道雄|Watanabe Michio}} (22 September 1940 - 14 November 2008) was a Sanese politician who served as the 43rd [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 15 May to 27 June 1991. His 43-day premiership was the shortest for a Sanese Prime Minister. Leading another short-lived coalition in the political chaos of 1991, Watanabe's government was formed after [[Masaaki Fukuda]]'s failed to pass an economic stimulus package. His own government lasted only a few weeks before collapsing, paving the way for [[Shigehara Honda]] to return as Prime Minister.

== Early life ==
Watanabe was born in [[Toyozawa]] in 1940, the son of a small business owner who operated a printing shop. His family's modest means required him to work from a young age, and he developed a reputation for diligence and reliability that would define his political career. He attended [[Okami University]], where he studied political science and became involved in student government.

After graduating in 1963, Watanabe worked briefly in local government before entering national politics. His early career was shaped by the rapid economic growth of the [[Go-go 8090]] era, and he developed a pragmatic, non-ideological approach to governance that emphasized competence over conviction.

== Political career ==
Watanabe was first elected to the House of Commoners in 1974, representing [[Toyozawa 2nd district]] as a member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]]. He built his political base through meticulous constituency service and a reputation for administrative competence, rising through the LDP ranks as a reliable technocrat rather than a factional operator.

During the [[Go-go 8090]] era, Watanabe served as Parliamentary Vice Minister of International Trade and Industry and later as Minister of State for Economic Planning, positions that allowed him to demonstrate his organizational skills without attracting the controversy that surrounded more ambitious colleagues. He was widely respected within the party for his ability to manage complex bureaucratic processes, but he was not considered a potential prime minister-his lack of charisma and his aversion to political confrontation made him an unlikely leader.

In 1989, Watanabe broke with the LDP over the party's handling of the emerging financial crisis, joining the newly formed [[Okaiken Shintō|Okaiken New Party]] (ONP) as one of its founding members. His defection was motivated by his conviction that the LDP's factional politics had made it incapable of addressing the banking crisis, and his move gave the ONP instant credibility as a party of experienced, principled reformers.

The political crisis of 1990-1991 changed the calculus of leadership. As the LDP's governing coalition disintegrated and a succession of short-lived governments failed to address the mounting economic crisis, the ONP emerged as a potential kingmaker. Watanabe, as the party's most experienced parliamentarian, was its natural candidate for the premiership when the opportunity arose.

== Premiership (May-June 1991) ==
=== Formation of the coalition ===
Watanabe was appointed prime minister on 15 May 1991, following the collapse of [[Masaaki Fukuda]]'s coalition government. Fukuda, who had led a Komeitō-centered coalition, had failed to pass an economic stimulus package through the Diet, and his government collapsed after just 48 days.

Watanabe's appointment was the product of frantic behind-the-scenes negotiations among the opposition parties. The ONP assembled a seven-party coalition-the largest in Okaiken's history-comprising the ONP, the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]], the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]], the [[Democratic Action Party of Okaiken|DAPO]], [[Komeitō]], the [[New Tohoku Democratic Party|NTD]], and the [[Social Democratic Party of Okaiken|SDPO]]. The coalition's only common ground was a shared desire to end the political chaos and address the economic crisis; on virtually every policy question, the seven parties disagreed.

=== The impossibility of governance ===
[[File:Watanabe Seven-Party Coalition 1991.png|thumb|right|200px|Watanabe's seven-party coalition, the largest in Okaiken's history, proved ungovernable]]
From the moment he took office, Watanabe faced an impossible situation. The Diet was deadlocked, with no party or coalition able to command a stable majority. The seven-party coalition was ungovernable: the OSP and SDPO demanded aggressive nationalization of failed banks, the PPO and DAPO favored market-based restructuring, the ONP and Komeitō sought a middle ground, and the NTD was primarily interested in securing agricultural subsidies for its rural base.

The economic crisis demanded immediate action-a stimulus package, banking reforms, and measures to address the collapsing real estate market-but the political paralysis made any legislative action impossible. Watanabe attempted to negotiate a compromise stimulus package that could attract support from all seven parties, but the negotiations stalled within days as each party demanded concessions that the others were unwilling to grant.

=== Collapse ===
On 27 June 1991, after 43 days in office, Watanabe announced his resignation. In a brief statement to the Diet, he said: `I accepted this office in the belief that a coalition of good faith could provide the stability the nation needs. I was wrong. The political system is paralyzed, and no coalition of seven parties can unblock it. The people deserve a government with a mandate, not a government by arithmetic.''

His resignation paved the way for [[Shigehara Honda]] to return as prime minister, leading a more stable center-right coalition that would govern for the next two years.

=== Domestic policy ===
Despite the brevity of his tenure, the Watanabe government attempted several domestic policy initiatives. The government introduced the Emergency Banking Stabilization Act of May 1991, which would have authorized the government to inject public funds into failing banks and to establish a bridge bank mechanism for the orderly resolution of insolvent financial institutions. The Act was a response to the growing crisis in the banking sector, where non-performing loans were threatening to destabilize the entire financial system. However, the Act was blocked by the coalition's internal divisions, with the OSP demanding full nationalization of failed banks and the PPO opposing any use of public funds.

The government also attempted to address the collapsing real estate market through the Real Estate Market Stabilization Act of June 1991, which would have provided tax incentives for property purchases and authorized the government to purchase distressed properties to prevent a fire-sale collapse. The Act was another victim of the coalition's paralysis, as the parties could not agree on the appropriate level of government intervention.

On the social policy front, the Watanabe government introduced the Emergency Employment Measures Act of May 1991, which provided funding for public works projects and job training programs to address the rising unemployment caused by the economic downturn. The Act was the only significant piece of legislation passed during Watanabe's tenure, as it was supported by all seven coalition parties as a minimal response to the economic crisis.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Watanabe government's foreign policy was severely constrained by the domestic political crisis, but the government did pursue several initiatives. The government continued Okaiken's active participation in the [[Adonian Community]], supporting the organization's efforts to coordinate a response to the global economic slowdown. The government also participated in the Adonian Community Monetary Policy Coordination Meeting of June 1991, which produced a joint statement committing member states to avoid competitive devaluations.

In the Shansian relationship, the Watanabe government maintained the existing diplomatic framework, but the territorial disputes over the northern islands continued to complicate bilateral relations. The government authorized the renewal of the Shansian-Sanese Fisheries Agreement, which provided for the cooperative management of fishing rights in the disputed northern waters.

The government also sought to strengthen Okaiken's economic ties with the Shendan states, dispatching a trade mission to negotiate bilateral investment agreements with several Shendan nations. The Shendan Economic Partnership Initiative, which had been launched under the Tokuyama government, was continued, and the government authorized increased funding for Shendan infrastructure projects.

== Later life and death ==
After his resignation, Watanabe remained in the Diet as a backbencher, continuing to represent Toyozawa 2nd district until his retirement from politics in 1995. He returned to Toyozawa, where he lived quietly and occasionally wrote opinion pieces for local newspapers on economic policy and political reform.

Watanabe died of heart failure in [[Toyozawa]] on 14 November 2008, at the age of 68. His funeral was attended by numerous political figures, including several former prime ministers.

== Legacy ==
Watanabe's premiership is remembered primarily for its brevity and its symbolic significance. His 43-day tenure remains the shortest for a Sanese Prime Minister, a record that underscores the depth of the political crisis that engulfed Okaiken in the early 1990s.

More importantly, Watanabe's resignation was an honest acknowledgment that the political system had broken down-a rare moment of candor in a political culture that prized appearances over reality. His failure to govern was not a personal failure but a structural one: the seven-party coalition was inherently ungovernable, and no prime minister could have made it work.

Watanabe himself viewed his premiership with characteristic pragmatism. In a 1996 interview, he said: `I was not a prime minister. I was a diagnostician. I proved that the patient was sick. The cure came later, under someone else's hand.''

